<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Learned About the History of Panama. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Learned About the History of Panama.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present personal opinions on a variety of topics. Interactive: Exchange beliefs, opinions, agreements, and disagreements politely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present and discuss in a polite manner a personal opinion on Panama's history (e.g., "Historians say the canal transformed Panama's economy." "I agree with that."). Can exchange opinions by supporting arguments with facts and responding to peers (e.g., "I believe the Panama Canal was crucial for global trade because it shortened maritime routes. What do you think about its environmental impact?"). Can agree or disagree with peers on historical topics (e.g., "I agree that independence shaped Panama's identity.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a projected image of a significant historical event in Panama (e.g., the construction of the Panama Canal or the signing of the Torrijos-Carter Treaties). Students are asked to briefly discuss with a partner what they see and what they know about it. Teacher prompts: "What historical event does this image represent? What do you think was its biggest significance for Panama?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express an opinion and ask for another's opinion using target language. For example: "This image shows the Panama Canal construction. I believe its construction was a huge engineering marvel that transformed global trade. What do you think about its legacy for the country's economy?" The teacher also models polite agreement/disagreement: "I agree with that because..." or "I understand your point, but I think..." The teacher pronounces "-ed" endings clearly (e.g., 'transformed' /d/, 'accomplished' /t/) and stresses 'research' (noun/verb).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'I believe the canal was crucial,' am I stating a fact or my personal view?" (Expected answer: Personal view/opinion)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask, 'What do you think?', do I want you to simply repeat what I said, or give your own idea?" (Expected answer: Your own idea)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio clips (1-2 minutes total) featuring two "historians" or "journalists" discussing an important historical event in Panama (e.g., the 1903 separation from Colombia or the transfer of the Canal). The clips include examples of reported speech, past simple, and passive voice. For instance, "Historians say Panama declared independence in 1903," or "The separation was reported as a turning point." The vocabulary used includes 'independence,' 'separation,' 'significance,' 'economy,' 'identity,' 'shaped,' 'transformed.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen to understand different perspectives on historical events, identifying key opinions and facts reported by others. This prepares them for interactive speaking tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the audio clips and identify the main historical event being discussed. (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students answer True/False questions based on the reported information (e.g., "Historian A said the economy was immediately boosted." T/F).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match each speaker's main opinion with a summary statement.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using past simple for historical events, reported speech (e.g., "Historians say the canal transformed..."), and passive voice (e.g., "The canal was completed..."). They will actively use vocabulary like 'history,' 'independence,' 'separation,' 'canal,' 'significance,' 'economy,' 'transformation,' 'shaped,' 'declared,' 'reported.' Pronunciation practice focuses on -ed endings, contrastive sounds like /s/ and /z/ in 'say' and 'was,' and noun/verb stress in 'research.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Prompts):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences orally, converting direct historical facts into reported speech or passive voice. Teacher provides prompts: "Fact: Panama declared independence in 1903." Student response: "Historians say Panama declared independence in 1903." Another prompt: "Fact: The French began the canal construction." Student response: "It is reported that the canal construction was begun by the French." The teacher monitors for correct grammar and pronunciation of /s/ and /z/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Information Exchange):</b> In pairs, students receive different sets of historical statements (e.g., "The canal brought economic diversity" or "Independence shaped Panama's identity"). They take turns reading a statement and then asking their partner for their opinion, using phrases like, "What impact did the construction of the Panama Canal have on the country's economy?" or "Do you think independence shaped Panama's national identity?" Partners respond using reported speech and personal opinions, e.g., "I've learned that many people believe the canal significantly boosted our economy, and I agree with that." Emphasis on stress for 'research' (verb).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Agree/Disagree):</b> Students remain in pairs. The teacher displays several controversial or debatable historical statements related to Panama (e.g., "The U.S. involvement in the canal's construction caused political tensions for years."). Students discuss each statement, expressing agreement or disagreement politely. They must use at least one piece of reported speech and one passive voice sentence in their discussion. For example, "I agree with that. Many historians say the political relationship was complex due to the construction period. What do you think?" The teacher encourages supporting arguments with facts (from previous lessons) and monitors for appropriate use of target vocabulary ('colonial,' 'revolutionary,' 'legacy') and -ed endings.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Historical Opinion Forum." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group chooses one significant historical event from Panama's history (e.g., "The Separation of Panama from Colombia," "The Construction of the Panama Canal," or "The Reversion of the Canal to Panama").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Each group prepares a short (3-4 minute) forum presentation where they briefly introduce the event, then each member presents a personal opinion on its impact or significance, supporting it with facts or reported information. They must use past simple, reported speech, and passive voice. After each group's presentation, the rest of the class can ask questions or politely challenge an opinion, initiating a brief debate. Students must use phrases for asking about events ("When did Panama separate from Colombia?") and sharing opinions ("I believe the Panama Canal was crucial because..."). Pronunciation of /ð/ and /θ/ in 'they' and 'think' should be paid attention to. Emphasis is on fluency, collaborative interaction, and supporting arguments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity simulates a real-world scenario of historians or experts discussing and debating historical interpretations, directly linking to "Learning to Research History" and "I Learned About the History of Panama."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Historical Opinion Forum" presentations and subsequent discussions. A rubric will be used to evaluate speaking skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in their group's "Historical Opinion Forum," presenting their individual opinion, supporting it, and engaging in the Q&A/debate session by responding to peers and asking clarifying questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of -ed endings, contrastive sounds, and word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'significance,' 'transformation,' 'legacy,' 'heritage,' 'revolution').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in presenting personal opinions and supporting arguments with facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of past simple, reported speech, and passive voice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Polite and collaborative interaction, including agreeing/disagreeing and asking for clarification.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short self-reflection form:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How clearly did I present my opinion and support it with historical facts or reported information today?" (1-5 scale)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I listen attentively to my peers and respond politely, even when I disagreed? Give an example."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned about discussing historical events with others today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific language (grammar or vocabulary) did I use effectively, and what do I need to practice more for future discussions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting student speaking and interaction in this lesson? (e.g., student engagement, target language use).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to more confidently present opinions, support them with facts, and exchange views politely? (e.g., more sentence frames, focused pronunciation drills).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on today's speaking practice. Students will be asked to write a persuasive paragraph or a short essay discussing their opinion on a historical event in Panama, using the facts and language structures practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>